WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:02.650
- [Instructor] I'm here at
the Khan Academy exercise

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.300
called Find One on the Number Line

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.190
and they're asking us to do exactly that.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.940
It says, "Move the dot to
one on the number line,"

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.730
and it's a little interactive dot

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.030
that I can move around.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.080
And so, let's think
about how I would do it

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.250
and I always encourage you,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.110
pause this video and see at least how

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.304
you would think about doing it

00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:23.780
or put your finger on the
screen where you think one is

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.379
and then we'll work through it together.

00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:26.500
All right.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.820
So they've told us where zero is

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.170
and they've told us
where seven fourths is.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.450
So one thing I could do is

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.799
I could say well how many
of these equal spaces

00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:39.320
does it take me to get
from zero to seven fourths.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.320
So let's see it's one, two,
three, four, five, six, seven.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.406
So if it takes seven equal
spaces to get to seven fourths,

00:00:50.406 --> 00:00:54.128
that means that each of
these spaces is one fourth

00:00:54.128 --> 00:00:57.090
because then it would be
one fourth, two fourths,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:02.090
three fourths, four fourths,
five fourths, six fourths,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.490
and seven fourths.

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.890
All right, we're making
some good progress.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.510
So where would one be?

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.270
Well, one would be four fourths.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:16.270
So we would go one fourth,
two fourths, three fourths,

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.580
and then four fourths.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.800
So that's where one is.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.580
So the important thing to
realize is the only way

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.280
we knew that each of
these, each of these gaps,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.353
or each of these spaces
from one hash to the next,

00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:32.666
the way that we knew that
each of those is a fourth

00:01:32.666 --> 00:01:36.150
is by saying hey look,
seven of those equal spaces

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.453
get us to seven fourths,

00:01:37.453 --> 00:01:39.621
so each of these must be a fourth.

00:01:39.621 --> 00:01:43.958
So four of those, four
fourths, would be equal to one.

00:01:43.958 --> 00:01:47.210
Let's do another example.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.220
So here, we're said,
we're told to move the dot

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.870
to one on the number line.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.420
So put your finger on where
that would be on the screen.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.640
Pause the video and do that.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.473
All right.

00:01:58.473 --> 00:02:00.130
In some ways this is a little bit easier

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.820
because they told us that going from zero

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.770
to the next little cross or
hash, I guess you could say,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.629
whatever you want to call
these things, is one sixth.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:10.899
So what is one?

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:14.560
Well, one or one whole is six sixths.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.560
So this is one sixth,
two sixths, three sixths,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.800
four sixths, five sixths,
and then six sixths.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.570
So there we go.

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.533
That is where is one
is on this number line.

